  "step_1": "The decision reason focuses on small probability outcomes (less than 20%) and applies a probability weighting rule: outcomes with probability < 20% receive a weight increased by 10 percentage points (i.e., their subjective weight = objective probability + 10 p.p.). In Lottery A, the 0 Euro outcome has 10% probability — this is below 20%, so it would be up-weighted to 20%. The -3000 Euro outcome has 90% probability, which is not small, so it remains at 90%. In Lottery B, the 0 Euro outcome has 55% probability (not small), and the -6000 Euro outcome has 45% probability (also not below 20%), so no outcomes in B qualify for up-weighting. Thus, only the 0 Euro outcome in A is affected by the reason’s rule. The weighted value under this reason would be: for A: (-3000 × 0.90) + (0 × 0.20) = -2700; for B: (-6000 × 0.45) + (0 × 0.55) = -2700. Both lotteries have the same weighted value (-2700), so the reason would be indifferent between A and B.",
  "step_2": "The individual notes that both lotteries have the same expected value and high loss probabilities. They emphasize that a loss is much more likely in A than in B (90% vs 45%), although the loss in B is larger (-6000 vs -3000). The individual justifies choosing B by stating that the difference in probabilities is large enough to outweigh the difference in loss magnitude. The deliberation focuses on the trade-off between probability and magnitude of loss, with greater emphasis on the lower likelihood of loss in B. No mention is made of low-probability outcomes receiving special attention or being weighted more heavily. The 10% outcome in A (0 Euro) is not discussed at all, nor is any psychological weighting of rare events mentioned.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires special attention to outcomes with probability < 20% and an explicit up-weighting of such probabilities by 10 percentage points. However, the individual does not mention or focus on the 10% outcome in A, which is the only one qualifying for such treatment. Instead, the justification centers on the overall likelihood of loss and the trade-off between probability and loss size — a comparison involving the dominant outcomes (90% and 45%) rather than the small-probability ones. The reasoning is more aligned with a probability-dominance or risk-aversion heuristic favoring lower loss probability, not a weighting of rare events. There is no evidence that the individual applied a rule that modifies small probabilities or focused on the low-probability outcome in A. Therefore, the information and rule central to the decision reason are absent from the verbal report.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
